In article <1992Oct21.143853.22255@decuac.dec.com>,
  mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. "Buddy can you spare a clue?" Ranum) writes:

> 
> 	I have heard the idea of making patches available assailed
> because of the quality control issue, "We can't do QA on patches, so
> someone installing one might actually make their machine worse."  The
> thing that a lot of folks who haven't been systems managers don't
> realize is that when something is broken, unless there is a workaround,
> things can't *get* a whole lot worse. A patch at least offers a chance
> at repairing the broken software, and any systems manager worth its
> salary is going to make sure the patch is reversible if possible.
>

   Precisely. If one needs a patch it's proof that QA wasn't done completely
enough on the original product, so refusing to make a fix available because
of QA concerns is pretty hollow. Of course one expects the patch to receive
a reasonable amount of testing by the developer before being released to
the public; this won't guarantee no nasty side effects, but should reduce
the probability of one. In any case this doesn't affect the issue of patches
over the Internet vs patches on tape via some other route; presumably they
will be the same patches with the same bugs present or absent.

> 
> Conclusion #1: It's OK to publish patches if you assume your customers
> 	aren't idiots.
> 

  Herein lies part of the problem - it's been my experience that Digital
generally works on the presumption that their customers are idiots :-)
To put that more diplomatically, Digital tends to assume that their customers
aren't interested in the technical aspects of system support and that they
want a "total service" solution for their systems. This leads to the CSC
support model as opposed to the FTP server model. There are clearly cases
for each model, but many of us are forced into the CSC model because it's
the only game in town. Even at that, the only time I've found the CSC effective
is when I've already identified the patch kit name ( via DSIN, Decuserve or
Usenet ) and ask them to send me a specific part number. They haven't been
much help with the research and problem solving aspect leading up to 
identifying the appropriate patches. Given this usage, an FTP site with
the patch kits and (ideally) an index would serve my needs much better.

Of course making the Internet model available would make customers less
reliant on CSC for every little problem, and could lead to lower revenues
from the service agreements one must pay for to access CSC. This could lead
some within Digital to resist such a move in order to keep revenues up.
This would be very unfortunate and is truly "not seeing the forest for the
trees", in that they would maintain a short term revenue stream while
alienating large segments of the customer base. If this short sighted view
must prevail then I'd suggest that we be given the option of paying for
access to this service IN LIEU of access to CSC ( not IN ADDITION ). I'd
much rather have access to an up-to-date patch server than to the folks at
the CSC.

> 
> 
> 	Lastly, of course, is the "good will" argument. This is
> the one that's always the hardest to pin down when presenting the
> issues to upper management. How does one quantify "commitment to
> UNIX" anyhow? 

  The original request didn't specify which O/S this was being considered
for. It's been clear from the responses that both Ultrix and VMS customers
want this service. Please,Please, PLEASE make it clear to the responsible
folks that this is not just a Unix issue, VMS customers have the same
problems and need the same kind of service.

