Good question ; according to two hardware manuals that I've read
(for a 5000/133 and a 5000/240) - the two serial lines can be
run between 50 bps and 202 kbps. 

Support beyond 19.2 kbaud is not provided by Ultrix or the on-chip
baud rate generator... ; however, if
you dig around the /sys hierarchy you will see that there are
indications of a work-around. For instance, in /sys/io/tc/sccreg.h ;
the register map of the SCC chip (A Zilog 8530) is layed out: 

The transmitter for each channel can be sourced from TxC,RxC,the
internal digital phased lock loop or the baud rate generator. Same
holds for the receiver.

Finally, if you check out the man pages on stty - you can set the
speed for all the common values 50-19.2 kbps AND two additional
settings called exta, extb. My guess is that the line discipline
supports user selection of the external clock pins through exta and
extb. You just need to get your modem to drive the clock pins on the
RS232C connector - seems that my Practical Peripherals 9600SA will
drives those pins if you select the option in the profile (AT&X2).
I havent tried this myself, but it may be a good start.



............ Jim Doyle



