to significantly increase security will have it's impact on usability.  If you
look at B1-B2 level securities, it basically makes the system unusable for 
normal applications unless they were specifically designed to operate in that
environment.  In most cases it appears that vendors take their existing OS
and modifiy it slightly to conform to government standards for security.  In
these cases I doubt that it makes much sense for the vendor to totally re-write
an OS for this purpose.  Not only would it not be cost effect, the person
wanting the system probably would be really upset that they now could not run
WordPerfect or Lotus or whatever...  Everyone loses.  So we see add on security
packages in which some do things like shadowing password files, etc.  I agree
