In article <1992Oct14.202136.3710@PA.dec.com>, grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L. Grant) writes:
> last march, i posted that DEC was going to do more with the Internet and
> asked customers to send me suggestions as to what they would like to see
> us do.  one of the most common requests was to make patches available over
> the Internet.  we are in the process of looking at doing this.  THIS IS
> NOT A PROMISE THAT IT WILL HAPPEN, IT IS A REQUEST FOR FEEDBACK AND HELP
> WITH JUSTIFICATION ON WHY WE SHOULD.
> 
> should DEC make patches available to customers over the Internet?

YES!  

> if so, why do you think this is a good idea?

There are patches that are routinely made available to customer support
centers.  Some of these are available via DSNlink.  Unfortunately, there are 2
problems with the current scenario: 
	1)  Not all customers have DSNlink (for a variety of reasons, 
		not being located in the USA is one of them). 
	2)  There can be a problem with obtaining patches quickly enough
		via the CSC 

I have personally made use of patches put up by a software vendor.  I find that
obtaining these is timely and suits the scenario we're typically put it.  Our
upgrades are normally performed during a change window on very early Sunday
mornings.  I do not expect our vendors to be easily available via phone during
these hours.  If an unforeseen problem arises, and I need a patch, it would be
nice (sometimes essential) to obtain an electronic copy in very short order. 
Our link to the Internet is significantly faster than any dial-in service we
can offer and I can probably scan an electronic copy of your patch listings
faster than I can phone a CSC, have them page someone else to scan the patches,
and upload the patch here.

