grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L. Grant) writes:

>last march, i posted that DEC was going to do more with the Internet and
>asked customers to send me suggestions as to what they would like to see
>us do.  one of the most common requests was to make patches available over
>the Internet.  we are in the process of looking at doing this.  THIS IS
>NOT A PROMISE THAT IT WILL HAPPEN, IT IS A REQUEST FOR FEEDBACK AND HELP
>WITH JUSTIFICATION ON WHY WE SHOULD.

>so i'd like to hear from you on these questions:
>should DEC make patches available to customers over the Internet?

YES!

>if so, why do you think this is a good idea?
In the past 8 months, I've needed, requested and received patches to
Ultrix 4.2 and 4.2A (for different reasons), and now Pathworks for Ultrix. 

Each patch entailed a call to software support, a 1 to 2 day wait (until
the patch arrived), and the downtime while the service or machine was
down for repair. Each instance was a reaction to something out of my
control. Offering patches and patch lists over the internet would :
1. stop the delay while I awaited the patches.  
2. help me manage systems in a pro-active manner, if I know what patches
are out there I can pretty well decide what is critical to the
funtioning of my systems. I'm held accountable by my users for system
uptime, offer me the tools to do my job -- I'll take what I need. 
	The existing situation is untenable if regression testing is
the primary concern. If you tell me the risks, and I make a mistake its
my problem. And of course, no one *has* to use the service.

DEC is well known for its support services, and from my experience does
it well in contrast to others. It seems that the delay incurred in getting
patches to the customer alone would justify the service. I can only
guess, but it also seems that the cost of making and shipping a patch
tape via UPS (i.e., the personnel, media, and delivery service) would be
higher than having a support engineer (or customer) use a patch server.

Since Gatekeeper is in place, UPS and tapes really do seem outdated. 

>any comments/suggestions on implementation of this would be welcome as well.

I find ftp patch-servers convenient and easy to use, where requests for
patches and patchlists are accepted. 


