ivie@cc.usu.edu (CP/M lives!) writes:
> Most DECmates that I am aware of aren't running WPS. My DECmate IIs run
> OS/278 and CP/M.

I doubt that's true.

> The DM III can support a hard drive. That package is called the DM III+; I'm
> not sure about upgrading the III to a III+. The III package, BTW, is the
> same as the VAXstation 2000 package; and they crammed a hard drive in there.
> In fact, the VS2000 sitting next to me has two hard drives in the package.

A III -> III+ upgrade consists of taking the III out of the box and putting
a III+ in.  8-)  Seriously, it's a complete new motherboard, and a daughter
board for the hard disk controller.  The expansion backplane and expansion
boards work in both models.  The power supply should be upgraded also, since
the RD31 and RX33 combined pull a bit more power than the RX50 in a III.  
It's not beyond the realms of possibility that later IIIs were III+s without
the HD controller.

> Yes, the II has a DOS emulator. It's not PC-compatible, though. That was
> also available for the III.

The II has either the APU or XPU boards available.  The APU consists of 
a Z80 processor, which uses the DECmate's own memory (64k 12 bit words; the
APU just uses the bottom 8 bits of each word) and allows the DECmate to run
CP/M.  The XPU has its own memory, a Z80 _and_ either an 8088 or 8086 (the
latter, I think), and can run MS-DOS, although it is not a PC klone by any
stretch of the imagination.

The III only ever had an APU board available for it, although an XPU did 
get to some stage in development.  The expansion boards for the II & III are
different, so the boards cannot be interchanged.

> WPS sucks, IMHO. I boot CP/M and use WordStar for word processing. Really
> serious stuff I pipe over to the VAX and spit through RUNOFF.

I disagree.  WPS-8 (as opposed to that abomination that runs on the VAX) has
the major redeeming characteristic that you can sit someone down in front of
a DECmate and have them productive in no time at all.  I'm yet to meet a 
wordprocessor quite like it in that respect.  It's showing it's age, but 
wipes the floor with WordsStar....

> Yes, the monitor is the VR201. You don't have to be picky about the keyboard
> (especially if you're not running WPS); I've even run an LK401 from mine.

You could put any composite video monitor onto a DECmate (or anything else
that uses the VR201).  The pinouts to the DB15 monitor cable are:

	1   *	blue return
	2   *	green return
	3   *	red return
	4	mono return
	5&6	ground
	7&8	+12 Vdc		(The LK201 gets its power from the host)
	9   *	blue video
	10  *	green video
	11  *	red video
	12	mono video
	13	monitor present (strapped to ground to indicate mon is present)
	14	keyboard transmit
	15	keyboard receive

	    * Not used with VR201 or mono systems

The LK201 cable pinout is (based on some multi-meter work -- no 
responsibility taken if smoke ensues!) appears to be:

	1	keyboard receive	(pin 14 on DB15)
	2	+12 Vdc			(pins 7&8)
	3	ground			(pins 5&6)
	4	keyboard transmit	(pin 15)

(Pin 1 is on the right if you look into the plug with the catch on top, ie:
				   __
				  |  |
				.|____|.
				|      |
				|_||||_|
				  4321

> Didn't like the touch of the LK401, though.

In the eight years or so that I've used LK201s, I've only ever tolerated
them.  I've got four of the silly things around here, three actually in
use.  Horrible things.

