ssmith@eos.arc.nasa.gov (Stephen Smith) writes:

>Each patch entailed a call to software support, a 1 to 2 day wait (until
>the patch arrived), and the downtime while the service or machine was
>down for repair. Each instance was a reaction to something out of my
>control.

	This is a key point, and I've been lurking back here waiting
for someone to make it first so I could throw in a few observations
of my own about broken-ness in software.

	I have heard the idea of making patches available assailed
because of the quality control issue, "We can't do QA on patches, so
someone installing one might actually make their machine worse."  The
thing that a lot of folks who haven't been systems managers don't
realize is that when something is broken, unless there is a workaround,
things can't *get* a whole lot worse. A patch at least offers a chance
at repairing the broken software, and any systems manager worth its
salary is going to make sure the patch is reversible if possible.

Conclusion #1: It's OK to publish patches if you assume your customers
	aren't idiots.


	A second problem I've heard raised with the patches issue
is that "some people may simply blindly install *every* patch even
if they don't need it, and will thereby maybe break that which is not
broken."  I don't know many systems managers who are that bored and
stupid, but there must be some, and doing so will be a learning
experience for them. On the other hand, it is *reasonable* to
expect that a customer might want to attempt to fix a problem
before it bites them!

Conclusion #2: If it's only a little broke, sometimes it's still
	worth fixing.


	Patches by themselves, in my opinion, are not all that is
necessary. *Ideally*, a vendor would publish patches, MD5 checksums
of the patches, and a permuted index of symptom:patch mappings. To
my knowledge nobody does the last of these, but it's probably the
most important one of the 3! Most customer support centers already
have the information in their problem tracking databases someplace.
A huge README file is nice, but nobody is going to want to wade
though all the verbiage.

Conclusion #3: If the vendor provides concise information about the
	importance of a patch, the customer can make an informed
	decision about whether or not to install it.


	I also have reason to suspect that patches can save a vendor
money. I've seen some (terrifying) breakdowns of how much a customer
call costs a service center, if it leaks past the first tier of
support and requires extra resources. A lot of the cost of solving a
customer problem is identifying it. It's probably to the vendor's
advantage if customers call and say, "Uh, I have bug #123871212322"
rather than, "my compiler doesn't work". That short-cuts the whole
process of diagnosis. Of course, the vendor still has to pay for
cutting a tape, mailing it, and so forth - a process that might be
more expensive than you'd like to think! Anyone who has read
comp.unix.ultrix for a while can tell you that there are lots of
savvy customers who call a support center, say, "I have the mumble
bug with /bin/fratz" and *STILL* wind up getting stuck on the phone
or having to wait for call-backs from support.

Conclusion #4: Publishing bug lists saves a support costs. Publishing
	patches saves more.


	Lastly, of course, is the "good will" argument. This is
the one that's always the hardest to pin down when presenting the
issues to upper management. How does one quantify "commitment to
UNIX" anyhow? What needs to happen is that customers need to explain
politely, yet firmly, that they, the customer, are always right.
They need to do this in written form to their sales reps, they need
to do it in letters to editors of trade rags and vendor owned
rags. They need to do this in the form of sales. They need to make
it requirements on RFPs. I bet you, if DOD RFPs all specified that
vendors must provide up-to-date patches on the internet, the next
time there was a $300Million proposal on the street, there would
be patches. Were I not working for a vendor, I would refuse to buy
a UNIX platform that didn't have a reasonable policy for making
patches available (see below). I'd thank them politely, explain my
requirements, and show them the door.

Conclusion #5: Sometimes putting a gun to someone's head will make
	them see the light.

A "reasonable patch availability policy":
	- Patches available via anonymous FTP from a stable internet
	source managed by the vendor.
	- Permuted index of symptom:patch available via anonymous FTP.
	- Cryptographic checksums of patch files included with patches.
	- Disclaimer of liability included with all patches, "do not
	take internally, etc."
	- Ability to submit bug reports via electronic mail. No requirement
	for anything more than automated acknowledgement of receipt. (Which
	is still better than what you get with most paper forms)

mjr.


