Mach: The OSF/1 Core Kernel
UCLA Extension
November 2-4, 1992

Nawaf Bitar
Eric Shienbrood

OSF/1 is the Open Software Foundation's first operating system
release.  It is based on Mach, a novel operating system intended
to recapture the original structural simplicity of UNIX.  Mach is
designed to provide portability, scalability, and advanced
functionality without sacrificing performance, and to provide support
for multiprocessors and distributed systems.  Monolithic versions
support compatibility with BSD systems by including the UNIX
compatibility code directly in the kernel.  Other versions support
multiple operating system personalities implemented as user-space
servers.

This course analyzes the Mach OSF/1 core kernel, addresses the vision,
philosophy and architecture, and offers a thorough study of the three
major subsystems that comprise the kernel: inter-task communication,
virtual memory management, and task/thread management.  Topics include
external memory management, copy-on-write optimizations, message passing,
thread scheduling and external processor allocation.  Following the
discussion of these fundamental mechanisms, the course describes how UNIX
compatibility is provided by building on these services.  Finally,
miscellaneous facilities provided under the Mach environment are described.

For more information, call the Short Course Program Office at (310) 825-3344;
FAX (310) 206-2815.


