In article <1992Oct14.202136.3710@PA.dec.com>, grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L.
Grant) writes:

> should DEC make patches available to customers over the Internet?

Yes.

> if so, why do you think this is a good idea?

It would reduce the people costs for distributing patches, both for customers,
and for DEC (I would assume).  How?  DEC sales staff would not have to "waste"
time handling requests for patches and tracking them down - a big win.  And for
customers, when a problem shows up, I'm sure it would be less time & effort to
download a list of available patches, and then download the needed patch if one
exists.

It would also improve the overall reliability of the installed base of DEC
machines, both by getting patches installed faster, and by getting them
installed *at all*.  Some sites live with "minor" problems (and grumble about
them) because of the overhead in finding & getting patches today.  Not that
it's difficult, just not always worth the time considering all the urgencies
that demand the attention of busy system managers.  Internet access would
lower the perceived (and actual) cost of finding/getting patches to "minor"
problems.

> any comments/suggestions on implementation of this would be welcome as well.

The usefulness of net-available patches will depend largely on how truly
accessible they are.  By accessible I mean:

    1)	It must be straightforward to find out "Does a patch exist for the
	problem I'm encountering?"  (This sounds like the harder part).

    2)	It must be straightforward to get the patch over the net (this sounds
	like the easier part).

Hopefully, old patches would be available too.  There are lots of VAXen out
there running 4.7, 5.2, etc.

I would also hope a mail-server would be available for those who can't just
telnet over to your system and FTP a patch.  For example, *lots* of VMS sites
are running DECUS UUCP (mine included), but not TCP/IP.  And it would be nice
for the mail-server to be as fully functional in problem-searching as an online
connection would be (albeit with slower turnaround to queries).  (This doesn't
sound easy, but I have confidence in DEC's ingenuity!  :-).

> i can't overstate this: we are CONSIDERING doing this, but are in no way
> PROMISING that we will.  but your input could help greatly.

Understood.
