>Is there a (non-documented) feature in the VT220, which sends back a string
>specified in a control-sequence?
>$ write sys$output "<some_magic_sequence>directory"
>and the VT220 (not a VMS-process or the terminal-driver!) sends the string
>"directory" back which is then evaluated by DCL.

	This used to be one of my favorite security holes under UNIX.
You grab the person's terminal type by doing a ps and checking the
environment, then see if the terminal type has a sendback string.
Most modern UNIXes default to keeping terminals unwriteable for just
this reason.
	How does VMS address this? I assume it's hard to send escape
sequences to someone else's terminal without privs of some type?

mjr.


