mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. "Buddy can you spare a clue?" Ranum) writes:

>David E. Cowley writes:

>>VAXes got a bad name in the last several years because they didn't keep 
>>getting faster at the same pace as the RISC boxes.  That's DEC's fault.
>>VMS, I think, got a bad name because it's associated with VAXes.

>	I never thought I'd hear myself sticking up for VAXes, but this
>is just wrong.

To the contrary, I think that David's point is exactly correct.  VAXen did
*not* keep pace with RISC CPUs.  And IMHO, this *most definitely* contribu-
ted to the bad rap VMS took.

>Right now, VAXes are running at some of the highest
>clock rates ever achieved for CISC machines. The VAX4000 is faster than
>a lot of RISC machines out there. Some of them are miracles of engineering.

Yes, but it's still slower than some.  And it came out later than the RISC
machines that it *did* bypass (David's point again).

There's also a telling point here, not germane to the central issue, in your
comment that "some of [the VAXes] are miracles of engineering".  This point
is no doubt true, but the lesson to be drawn is that the corresponding RISC
CPUs are *not* miracles of engineering, but rather straightforward application
of design principles.  This, to me, means that it is almost certain that RISC
CPUs would *always* have remained ahead of VAXes (assuming that VAXes had
survived).

