> if so, why do you think this is a good idea?

(1) First, your major competitor does it already (and has been for a
while).  Look in ftp.uu.net:/systems/sun/sun-fixes.  When I was sysadmin-ing
a network of Suns, I found this resource to be indispensible.

(2) When Sun did this, it raised my confidence in Sun that they were serious
about fixing bugs, or at least in acknowledging problems.  In fact, Sun
regularly put out a thick book of "known fixed and not-fixed bugs in SunOS
and Sun unbundled software".  Again, this was book was invaluable.  If
you're worried about losing customer confidence if you publish a list of
bugs:  don't be.  When I looked at Sun's thick list of bugs, I didn't say
"boy, Sun software is shitty".  No, I said, "wow, Sun is very thorough in
listing all their bugs - I like that."  A thin book would've made me
suspicious.

(3) Whenever I encountered a problem (and most times, even before I
encountered a problem), I could search to see if this was a known bug.  The
book put out by Sun of known bugs was especially useful.  Even if a vendor
doesn't have a patch or a fix for the problem, knowing about bugs beforehand
instead of rudely finding out about them when your boss needs the system
running Right Now Dammit can save jobs and lives.

(4) Being able to get the patches right then and there intead of calling
your sales rep, playing an n-day phone tag with him/her, trying to convince
the rep that there is a problem, having the rep search and find a patch, then
arranging for a time for the rep to physically bringing a tape, waiting for
rep to get here (while I'd rather be doing work at various locations instead
of wasting company time and money just sitting around), having the rep's car
tire blow and thus delaying it a day more (yes, this actually happened), etc.
This is simply ridiculous.

If DEC does make patches available over the net, it would increase my
confidence in DEC software support immeasurably.  As a customer, I beg and
plead you to go ahead with this!

