In article <1992Oct16.181410.25604@PA.dec.com>, grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L. Grant) writes:
-
- should DEC make patches available to customers over the Internet?

YES, please.
 
- if so, why do you think this is a good idea?

because it is often extremely difficult to organize system upgrades. Having
access to patches with a limited scope would make testing out easier.

- any comments/suggestions on implementation of this would be welcome as well.

The big problems are patch interdependence and organisation. Should a 
particular patch contain:

   (a)  all patches since the last major system release ?
   (b)  just those that it is known to depend upon.
   (c)  just the isolated fix.

(a) needs a lot of disk space and bandwidth, and has the problem that if 
a site felt the need to develop a workaround for a problem that is incompatible
with the patch for it, then it would be barred from installing any later patch.

(b) is almost like (c) in that interpatch dependencies are sometimes very
difficult and expensive to find.

(c) has the additional hazard that people need to decide what set of patches 
they want, and (schock horror) keep records on which were installed.

There is also the fact that sometimes minute changes can create massive patch 
sets. Say the date() function was parsing the TZ environment variable 
incorrectly. A minor patch, but virtually all binaries need to be relinked
or replaced (assuming no shared libraries).

But in many cases, a patch is only a small number of replacement files.

It would also be execellent if in case of (b) or (c) above software to read
patch dependencies and create a set of required and suggested patches
given a desired one would be published. The software to create such patches
might actually be a product some people would want to buy ...

                                     Thomas

