Actually, DNI 7.0 behaves fairly reasonably in small DECNET configurations,
but has serious problems when trying to be used in large DECNET systems.
The trouble is that for some reason that I just can't fathom, Sun decided
to store the node database (the DECNet equivalent of /etc/hosts) in KERNEL
MEMORY (mbufs!).  

Here's the netstat output on a machine with the full NSI DECNet node database
loaded (NSI DECnet = the old SPAN network):


7735/7968 mbufs in use:
	32 mbufs allocated to data
	14 mbufs allocated to packet headers
	100 mbufs allocated to socket structures
	135 mbufs allocated to protocol control blocks
	15 mbufs allocated to routing table entries
	7 mbufs allocated to socket names and addresses
	2 mbufs allocated to interface addresses
	7430 mbufs allocated to <mbuf type 64>
11/32 cluster buffers in use
1028 Kbytes allocated to network (95% in use)
0 requests for memory denied
0 requests for memory delayed
0 calls to protocol drain routines


Sun's have problems when running with this many mbufs, and occaisionally it
crashes.  We load piles of RAM in the machine, so user processes are pretty
much unaffected.  The mbuf type 64 mbufs are where DNI sticks the node 
database info when it starts up.

I understand that Sun FINALLY fixed this with DNI 8.0, but alas you need
Solaris 2.0 to run it, and that's a mess to support.  This machine above
turns out to be one of the major mail relays we run from the DECNet Internet
to the IP Internet.  This problem with mbuf use has proven so troublesome
that we're probably going to replace that system with a DECstation running
Ultrix (Yuk!) and DEC's DECNet for Ultrix (which does something halfway
reasonable with the node database).  

I sure wish Sun would have fixed this without requiring Solaris 2.0.  At
least I don't have to run OSF/1 on the DECstation...  1/2 :-)

						Thanks,
						   Milo

PS Usual disclaimers about not representing the official opinion of the
United States Government apply...



