In article <BvtKvK.3I2@mtholyoke.edu> jbotz@mtholyoke.edu (Jurgen Botz) writes:
>
>I wouldn't make the mistake of underestimating WNT's importance or MS's
>muscle, but on the other hand, when you _do_ look at the demographics of
>the situation, you see millions of PCs with not enough memory or disk space
>to even think about running WNT.  Who is MS targeting?  According to their
                                   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>hype, the "power users".  Who are the power users?  Well, the scientific
>community is a very important sector of what the marketeers call "power
>users."
>

NT is targetted squarely at the market Unix addresses IN BUSINESS, ie one of
networked machines with centralised file servers. I hate to break this to you
but the growing market is business. This is because the scientific community
already has it's (albeit old?) machines installed, whereas many businesses
are just starting to invest in a big way. This is who Microsoft are after, the
businesses who need to network, but are currently running DOS. It's got to
be the biggest computer market, and will be for probably at least five years
as every business suddenly finds they can't control their information flow
without a server.

I should qualify what I mean by business here. What I am thinking of is
companies of 100 people or less, who have just cottoned on to the fact that
is can be quite convenient to be able to send electronic mail. These folks
are not power users, they use DOS day to day for simple invoicing, parts,
memos, etc. They do not yet have an information system as such, they just
realise that DOS is lacking, and lo and behold, look what's coming over the
horizon, a saviour, NT!

What they don't know is that, if they thought learning DOS was tricky, just
wait until NT hits the fan. The salesman/papers/magazines haven't quite
explained this side of the story to them. If you want to make some money,
get into network administration next year...

I don't see that NT is going to be any better than Unix, since it aims to
fulfill exactly the same niche, and it's got 15 years to catch up on.
However, Microsoft will have learnt from Unix's mistakes, and will also
have a customer base who are virtually on their knees and begging.


