In article <Bw02qs.831@fulcrum.bt.co.uk>, rct@fulcrum.bt.co.uk (Richard Taylor) writes:
> I don't see that NT is going to be any better than Unix, since it aims to
> fulfill exactly the same niche, and it's got 15 years to catch up on.
> However, Microsoft will have learnt from Unix's mistakes, and will also
> have a customer base who are virtually on their knees and begging.

In addition to learning from Unix's mistakes, and in addition to the
considerable installed base of DOS (and especially MS-Windows) users, some of
the other advantages of NT will be:

    -	Learned from VMS's mistakes (from all appearances, NT is Dave Cutler's
	new, improved VMS)
    -	Multiple operating environments, e.g. DOS, Windows, Posix, OS/2
    -	Multiple file systems
    -	Multiple protected subsystems in general

Technical advantages aside, the fact that NT is *the* upgrade path for
MS-Windows is quite enough to make it a brilliant success.  No flames from
OS/2 advocates, please.
