In article <1992Oct6.102901.13845@rks.se>, vaxmith@rks.se writes:

> We are planning to move [a VMS ODS-2] drive to a Ultrix-machine...

It's been done, but the code was never made to run on recent versions of
ULTRIX and is no longer available.  It was a read-only implementation of
ODS-2 under GFS (the g-node file system of ULTRIX).

Since you can reasonably use only sequential files this way (the only
kind UNIX recognizes), use VMSTAR on VMS to create a tarfile of all the
contents of the disk, then move the disk to UNIX and reinitialize it,
then restore the tarfile.  At this point you'll have all your files and
you'll be able to both read and write to the disk.

VMSTAR is available on gatekeeper in the form of a source kit that
builds on VMS.

An alternative, of course, is to put NFS software on the VMS machine and
simply serve the files to the ULTRIX machine.  Digital's "TCP/IP Services
for VMS" works fine for this.

