The research group I'm in here at UC Berkeley is interested in running
Windows NT on DEC Alpha's. I've done some preliminary investigation
and I've found something that troubles me. This is that it appears that
it will be difficult to run an X-server in "native mode" under WNT.
(By "native mode" I mean an X-server that has complete control
of the display and that isn't running as some kind of subwindow under
Windows, which is the way the several current products work).

If you look at the block diagrams of WNT you see that there are a
number of "protected subsystems" that run on top of the WNT
Executive. Examples of these are the Win32 and POSIX subsystems.
The diagrams I've seen make it appear that these subsystems are
all equal. At first glance, I though it would be possible to run
an X-server (and Xlib) under the POSIX protected subsystem, or maybe
to even create a new protected subsystem that I'd call the X-server
protected subsystem.

It turns out that these assumptions are wrong, according to people
I've talked to at DEC. What appears to be true is that the Win32
subsystem is more equal than others and always controls the display
for all protected subsystems. Also, the POSIX subsystem does its I/O
through a tty emulator, which is under the control of the Win32 subsystem.
Furthermore, Microsoft hasn't released the API necessary to implement
a protected subsystem.

For a group like ours, which is actually looking favorably at WNT,
these details are very disappointing. It appears that the X community
will be ignored when Windows NT for Alpha comes out, assuming the
eXcursion product isn't acceptable (which may or may not be true).
People who tend to look for the negative may start accusing DEC of
another flip-flop since the X-based environment that DEC has been
promoting so heavily will be a second-class citizen under WNT.

I'd enjoy hearing people's comments about this, especially any corrections
to the way I've interpreted the facts.

