|> >This is that it appears that
|> >it will be difficult to run an X-server in "native mode" under WNT.
|> 
|> 	Is this a bug or a feature?
|> 
|> 	No, seriously - If you consider that X windows' installed
|> base is a drop in the bucket compared to ms-windows', Microsoft
|> would be crazy to put engineering effort into making X a priority.

I tend to agree with this assertion, but do feel the need to add something.
The *primary* feature that the X11 environment provides over most other
commercial window/graphics systems is the network transport layer. Much of
the rest of the environment in X11 can be found in most other commercial
window/graphics systems (such as Windows/NT).

If Windows/NT were modified to provide an implementation of the ANSI-speced
network transport layer (based on that used by X11).... Well, you get the
idea. Rumor has it that there are a couple of companies out there trying to
do exactly this (well, maybe not the ANSI-speced flavor).

The only reason that I could see to justify NOT doing this is that Microsoft
believe that the transport should not be at the graphic primitives level
(ala X11), but rather higher up in the application layer (or maybe at the
graphic object layer?). Given the IP stack available on Windows/NT, making
network based programs that use a transport in the application layer can be
done today. The missing element some form of API or *standard* that would
allow a large and diverse set of applications to use/share/communicate via
this mechanism.

- Marc
