San Jose Mercury News
Thursday, Sept. 24, 1992
Business


	Sun covertly backed suit against rival group
   			By Lee Gomes
     
     Sun Microsystems inc. has been secretly backing a tiny software
firm's lawsuit against a group of Sun's industry rivals, an arrangement
that one legal expert said could be unethical.
     Sun's help,. disclosed in court documents last week, was
acknowledged by Sun on Wednesday and was estimated to reach $7 million
in legal fees.
     In a brief statement, after which a spokeswoman declined to answer
additional questions, Sun said it was aiding Addamax Corp., a small
Illinois software firm, in a lawsuit against the Open Software
Foundation, a non-profit consortium of Sun's major competitors in the
workstation industry.
     The foundation is creating a version of the UNIX operating system.
Sun is endorsing a competing effort, leading to one of the fiercest
wars in the computing industry in recent years.
     Addamax filed an antitrust lawsuit in April 1991 to contest the
foundation's decision to use one of Addamax's competitors as a
subcontractor.  The suit, filed in Massachusetts, listed Addamax as the
sole plaintiff and named Hewlett-Packard Co. and Digital Equipment
Corp. as added defendants.
     Industry observers said Sun might not want to be publicly
associated with the lawsuit for public relation reasons:  It wouldn't
want to be seen as using the court system - rather than the marketplace
- - - to go after a competitor, especially one that stands for "open systems."
     Open systems is non-proprietary software that allows computers
from various manufacturers to work together.
     Addamax admitted to the assistance from Sun only last week.  It
refused to answer pre-trial questions from the foundation's lawyers
about outside help until forced to do so by the judge.
     A foundation spokeswoman said Sun "has put $7 million" into the Addamax suit.
     "We're not suggesting anything is illegal," she said.  "We're just
surprised and disappointed to find out that this is one the realities of
the case, especially when Sun says it is a proponent of open systems."
      In its brief statement, Sun said it was asked by Addamax to
provide a loan guarantee to help fund the lawsuit and "because Addamax
has been a vendor to the company and appears to be raising serious
issues of importance to the industry, we agreed to do so."
     The Sun spokeswoman declined to answer questions about the case,
and it was not clear whether Sun had given money to Addamax or simply
guaranteed a loan to cover the legal bills.
     Sun's actions were questionable,m said Barbara Babcock, a
professor at the Stanford Law School, who said they could spell trouble
for the company.
     While simply providing a loan guarantee would be considered less
offensive than actually paying Addamax's legal bills,  Sun's behavior
"raises some serious questions of ethics and deception that the court
will probably want to have answered," she said.
     Especially disturbing, Babcock said, was that Addamax refused to
divulge Sun's help until being ordered to by the judge.  "Whenever
someone reacts that way, you know they have something to hide," she said.
     Babcock said a judge would have sereveral options in handling the
matter ranging from letting it pass to levying penalties against Sun,
Addamax or Addamax's lawyers.

