	I think your wrong. VMM is to allow PALcode to make
the 21064 appear like it's a VAX chip or for that matter like any other
CPU that you wish to imitate , rather than run native 21064.

	The way that OSF/1 (gold) and Windows NT would run together
is that they both use a common micro kernel (Mach 3.0). With Mach the
operating system env appears to the micro kernel as a server process. Mach
is a large cross bar switch for message handling. The OS is out there with
the application programs and gets a share of the CPU just the same.

	I thought VMS was going to be build on Mach 3.0 so that it to could
move away from being VAX only. I had been recently lead to believe that the
ALPHA version of VMS was still not Mach3.0 based.

